  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published molecular study test whether modular recurrent designs keep performance while gaining tractability.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that recurrent architectures with modular or reducible structure can retain performance while becoming simpler to analyze or implement. A lateral predictive coding preprint reports that optimal networks reduce response time near its lower bound and that modular wiring matches all-to-all performance. A second preprint uses a two-site cavity method to show that covariances in random nonlinear recurrent networks match a linear network driven by independent residual noise. The published RNCRN study extends modular recurrent design to molecular systems, proposing a trainable architecture that approximates arbitrary biochemical dynamics. New across these items is evidence that modularity need not cost performance, that nonlinear recurrent statistics admit a linear description, and that recurrent computation can be instantiated in chemical reaction networks.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-28"
}
